+## Heat/Cool Conditioning
+
+When a task requires a heat or cool conditioning step, perform that step directly at the conditioning appliance while holding the exact target.
+
+1. Go to the conditioning appliance (for example, microwave or stoveburners for heat, fridge for cool).
+2. Ensure the exact target is in hand. If it was stored inside the appliance, take it out first.
+3. Issue the heat or cool action itself while holding the target. Do not insert the target into the appliance and then repeatedly close and open it, and do not remove and reinsert the target before conditioning.
+4. Open the appliance only when you must retrieve the target from inside it or when the heat/cool action is not offered.
+5. After conditioning, go straight to the requested destination and place the target. The conditioning appliance is not the destination unless the task says so.
+
+Continue to reject near-match categories at the conditioning step.
+
 ## General Non-Desklamp Retrieval, Cleaning, and Placement
 
 For ordinary put, clean, or transfer tasks where no desklamp is required, use this procedure instead.
 
-1. Identify and search for the exact requested target. If it is not visible, run a single-pass sweep that spreads across distinct receptacle families rather than exhausting or looping over one type. Open each closed container (cabinet, drawer, fridge, etc.) once upon reaching it and read its contents; if it lacks the target, continue to a receptacle you have not yet inspected. Use your most recent observations to avoid returning to a receptacle you just confirmed empty. When one family (for example drawers) yields nothing, switch to a different uninspected family (such as cabinets, shelves, countertops/other surfaces, or appliances) before revisiting any earlier location. Keep the sweep within the step budget so that unopened containers and uninspected surfaces are checked before any loop back. Do not repeatedly check inventory while carrying nothing. Continue until the exact target is found or all plausible candidate receptacles have been inspected once.
+1. Identify and search for the exact requested target. If it is not visible, run a memory-independent family-rotation sweep based on the current admissible go-to actions (and the room description if still available). Treat candidate receptacles as families: cabinets, drawers, shelves, countertops/diningtables/sidetables, and appliances such as fridge, microwave, sinkbasin, stoveburners, and toaster. Within each family, advance through uninspected receptacles in ascending index order (cabinet 1, cabinet 2, and so on), opening and reading each closed container once and moving on immediately when it lacks the target. Do not exhaust one large family before checking another: after an ordered pass through part of a family, or when the current family has no promising uninspected member, switch to a genuinely uninspected family. Prefer compact open surfaces and then appliances/fridge before returning to later members of a large closed-container family, and continue the same fixed order. Because only the two most recent observations are available, rely on this fixed family/index order rather than memory to guarantee progress; never return to a receptacle already seen empty and never cycle among the same few locations. Continue until the exact target is found or all plausible families have been sampled once. When the step budget is tight, favor new unvisited families or containers over repeats and keep enough steps to complete any required conditioning and final placement. If all plausible families have been inspected once without the target, do not substitute a near-match or keep cycling.
 2. Manipulate, condition, or place only the exact requested target category. Before acquiring, cleaning, cooling, heating, or placing an object, confirm that its exact category name matches the requested target. Do not complete the task with a functionally similar but differently named object: treat a knife as distinct from a butterknife, a soapbottle as distinct from a soapbar, a cup as distinct from a mug, and a spraybottle as distinct from a soapbottle. If only a near-match is present at the current location, leave it untouched and continue searching for the exact category; never clean, cool, heat, or place a near-match in place of the requested object. If the exact target has not yet been found, continue the systematic search rather than completing the transfer with a non-target object.
 3. Acquire the exact target. If the task requires the object to be clean, carry it to an appropriate cleaning receptacle such as a sinkbasin and perform the clean action. If no cleaning is requested, skip the cleaning detour.
 4. Go directly to the requested destination. Open it first if it is closed, then place or move the target there. Do not detour to unrelated receptacles or release the target before the requested placement is completed.
